    "type": "question",
    "label": "26",
    "prompt": "Find $\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x\\sin 2x)$, and hence find $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/4}x\\cos 2x\\,dx$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x\\sin 2x)=\\sin 2x+2x\\cos 2x$; $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/4}x\\cos 2x\\,dx=\\dfrac{\\pi-2}{8}$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{d}{dx}(x\\sin 2x)&=\\sin 2x+2x\\cos 2x.\\end{align*}$$\nRearranging,\n$$\\begin{align*}x\\cos 2x&=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{d}{dx}(x\\sin 2x)-\\sin 2x\\right).\\end{align*}$$\nHence\n$$\\begin{align*}\\int_0^{\\pi/4}x\\cos 2x\\,dx&=\\frac12\\left[x\\sin 2x+\\frac12\\cos 2x\\right]_0^{\\pi/4}\\\\&=\\frac12\\left[\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\cdot1+\\frac12\\cdot0\\right)-\\left(0\\cdot0+\\frac12\\cdot1\\right)\\right]\\\\&=\\frac\\pi8-\\frac14\\\\&=\\frac{\\pi-2}{8}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 39
